WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.640
An aquatic bromance, horrors of the deep,&nbsp;
and an AI daddy? The long-awaited trailer for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.720
"Aquaman and the Lost Kingdom" is packed with&nbsp;
details that might have swum right past you.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.760
Not only is Aquaman back for "Aquaman and the&nbsp;
Lost Kingdom," but the DC sequel expands the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:19.211
fish-friendly hero's wardrobe, putting a spin on&nbsp;
the classic blue stealth suit from the comics.

00:00:19.211 --> 00:00:24.420
The blue suit debuted in 1986's "Aquaman" #1,&nbsp;
and its ability to allow Aquaman to blend in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.920
while underwater proved to be a contrast to&nbsp;
his iconic orange and green outfit. As with all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.360
superhero costume changes, the blue camouflage&nbsp;
suit has its fans and detractors. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.960
it's hard to not appreciate a nod&nbsp;
to a deep piece of DC Comics lore.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.620
It seems that Jason Momoa and James Wan&nbsp;
are fans of the blue camouflage suit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:44.400
as the "Aquaman and the Lost Kingdom" trailer&nbsp;
gives a great look at the hero's new costume.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.320
In the trailer, we see Aquaman donning his new&nbsp;
dark-blue suit, using its camouflage technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.160
which seemingly makes him invisible, to&nbsp;
break into the desert prison housing Orm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.240
Although it probably won't be around for&nbsp;
long, simply referencing Aquaman's comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:01.740
book wardrobe change will surely be enough to&nbsp;
satisfy diehard fans. And it's always possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.280
he's wearing the suit in other scenes in&nbsp;
the trailer. You just...can't see him.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:11.640
At long last, "Aquaman and the Lost Kingdom"&nbsp;
sees the return of DC's favorite couple. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.840
maybe not exactly. For those wondering,&nbsp;
Amber Heard is definitely reprising her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.900
role as the Queen of Atlantis. Her&nbsp;
highly publicized defamation trial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.980
involving ex-husband Johnny Depp caused&nbsp;
a backlash, with fans even calling for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.788
her replacement. But lo and behold, she is&nbsp;
in at least one shot of the new trailer.

00:01:26.788 --> 00:01:29.460
The majority of the footage shows&nbsp;
Arthur's relationship with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:32.985
reluctant half-brother Orm as they team&nbsp;
up to save Atlantis from Black Manta.

00:01:32.985 --> 00:01:34.740
"Good job, old brother. High five."

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:37.020
"Do not call me brother."

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:42.240
So, where does that leave Mera? It's hard to say.&nbsp;
In a succession of increasingly fast-paced action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.320
shots, Mera appears in only one brief moment,&nbsp;
where she seems to be trying to break through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.820
window underwater. Even when Arthur appears happy&nbsp;
with his baby, Mera is hardly in the picture.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:55.260
James Wan has admitted to Entertainment Weekly&nbsp;
that the film doesn't focus on Arthur's romantic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:59.520
entanglements, but instead on the two brothers.&nbsp;
With a scaled-down role in the movie's story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.600
it makes sense then why she doesn't appear&nbsp;
prominently in the trailer. By skipping ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.500
four years, viewers will have missed out on a&nbsp;
lot of the development between Arthur and Mera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.020
but hopefully, we at least get&nbsp;
to see her be with her family.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.940
One of the most appealing aspects of the world&nbsp;
of "Aquaman" is that there's no shortage of odd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.780
creatures and humanoid hybrids that can come&nbsp;
into play when Arthur Curry is in Atlantis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.920
The trailer for "Aquaman and the Lost Kingdom"&nbsp;
offers a glimpse of plenty of new additions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:26.823
including some hammerhead shark people flanking&nbsp;
Arthur and Orm while they're underwater.

00:02:26.823 --> 00:02:29.400
These could very well be some&nbsp;
associates of King Shark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.196
who entered the DCEU in "The Suicide Squad."

00:02:32.265 --> 00:02:34.425
"New dumb friends!"

00:02:34.425 --> 00:02:40.020
But according to some reports, the movie&nbsp;
is set to feature the character of Karshon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.660
another shark from DC comics who rapidly evolved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.382
to human-like intelligence and telepathy&nbsp;
after being hit with a dose of radiation.

00:02:46.382 --> 00:02:49.920
So even though they look similar, we&nbsp;
wouldn't count on King Shark showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.440
up. He might be too weird&nbsp;
even for an Aquaman movie.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.440
The "Aquaman and the Lost Kingdom" trailer holds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.740
plenty of references to the aquatic&nbsp;
superhero's past, but one of its more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:02.220
veiled references may actually be a nod&nbsp;
to the real-life history of his actor.

00:03:02.653 --> 00:03:08.593
"Four years ago, I was basically&nbsp;
unemployed. A wanderer with no home."

00:03:08.593 --> 00:03:12.000
This line about the hero's history&nbsp;
actually mirrors that of Jason Momoa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.760
who has revealed in interviews that&nbsp;
he struggled with a prolonged period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:20.100
of unemployment after his breakout stint as Khal&nbsp;
Drogo on "Game of Thrones." He once told InStyle:

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:24.420
"I couldn't get work. It's very challenging when&nbsp;
you have babies and you're completely in debt."

00:03:24.682 --> 00:03:27.900
"Where are you going to put Drogo in a comedy?&nbsp;
It's like, where the hell do you put him?"

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.380
A bit later in the trailer, Aquaman&nbsp;
declares that he finally got a job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:35.580
as the king of Atlantis. In a similar vein,&nbsp;
Momoa's struggle to find work improved when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:39.900
he was cast as Aquaman in the DCEU.&nbsp;
Momoa's final appearance on "Game of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:45.240
Thrones" was in 2012, while his debut as&nbsp;
Aquaman came in 2016 — a four-year gap.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:50.400
Is this an intentional reference to Momoa's story?&nbsp;
Maybe not, but it's hard to ignore the parallels.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:55.140
At least one thing is clear from the "Aquaman&nbsp;
and the Lost Kingdom" trailer: director James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:59.520
Wan is letting his horror roots show more than&nbsp;
ever. While the first "Aquaman" movie had some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.020
scary sequences of its own, the horror dial is&nbsp;
clearly getting cranked up to 11 for the sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:09.000
as viewers can see what are ostensibly zombified&nbsp;
Atlanteans rising up to do battle with Atlantis.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.080
What casual fans may not have picked up on is that&nbsp;
Wan appears to finally be getting an opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:17.220
to make the horror-tinged Atlantis story that&nbsp;
he has long desired. At one point in time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:21.480
the esteemed horror director was slated to direct&nbsp;
an "Aquaman" spin-off called "The Trench," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.780
would focus on the sinister beings that&nbsp;
Aquaman faces off against in his first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.460
movie. Unfortunately, the project was canned,&nbsp;
leaving eager fans worried that Wan's take on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:34.020
a more overtly horror-inspired DC project would&nbsp;
not come to fruition. Thankfully, this latest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:38.248
trailer suggests that he managed to make his&nbsp;
wish come true, albeit in a different form.

00:04:38.248 --> 00:04:41.100
It seems that "Aquaman and the Lost&nbsp;
Kingdom" may be drawing more from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:44.400
Wan's scrapped "The Trench" movie than&nbsp;
some may have expected. The director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.640
once revealed that the canceled film was&nbsp;
stealthily going to feature Black Manta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:51.720
whose gruesome story is now front-and-center&nbsp;
here. He has also teased that the "Aquaman"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:56.582
sequel will bring back other concepts from "The&nbsp;
Trench." Speaking to ComicBook.com, Wan said:

00:04:56.582 --> 00:04:58.560
"Listen, I feel like everything I come up with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:01.740
everything I do, if I come up with&nbsp;
something that I don't end up using,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:05.192
that doesn't mean that I cannot be&nbsp;
inspired to use that in a different way."

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.760
Perhaps one of the most interesting&nbsp;
details in the "Aquaman and the Lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.780
Kingdom" trailer happens as the first look&nbsp;
wraps up. Pay close attention to the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:16.320
moments as the credits for the film come&nbsp;
up and viewers will see that Jason Momoa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:20.580
has received a "Story by" credit alongside&nbsp;
James Wan, David Leslie Johnson-McGoldrick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:24.540
and Thomas Pa'a Sibbett. While Momoa didn't&nbsp;
write the script, "The Lost Kingdom" does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:28.936
feature story ideas from the actor. During an&nbsp;
interview with The Hollywood Reporter in 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.936 --> 00:05:32.700
Momoa expressed just how excited he was to&nbsp;
have played a part in the sequel's narrative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:37.458
revealing that he wrote a 50-page treatment for&nbsp;
the project. Teasing the sequel, the actor said:

00:05:37.458 --> 00:05:38.718
"It's got my heart in it."

00:05:38.718 --> 00:05:41.820
How did the writing process start?&nbsp;
In an interview with Drew Barrymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:44.640
Momoa candidly discussed what it&nbsp;
was like to cook up the sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.780
revealing that he worked on the first treatment,&nbsp;
and Wan and Johnson-McGoldrick finished it off.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:56.460
"After we ended the first one, I went in with my&nbsp;
writing partner and we dreamed up the second one."

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:01.080
Black Manta is more powerful than ever in&nbsp;
"Aquaman and the Lost Kingdom" thanks to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:04.980
acquisition of the Black Trident. However,&nbsp;
that may not be the only upgrade he gets in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:08.940
the sequel. It may be a bit of a stretch,&nbsp;
but around the 0:52 mark of the trailer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:13.013
he gets inside a vessel that could be a loose&nbsp;
adaptation of the Mecha Manta from the comics.

00:06:13.013 --> 00:06:16.467
"I'm gonna kill Aquaman and&nbsp;
destroy everything he holds dear."

00:06:16.467 --> 00:06:20.940
The Mecha Manta is a fairly new addition to Black&nbsp;
Manta's arsenal in the comics, coming into play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:26.760
originally in "Aquaman" Volume 8 #51, which came&nbsp;
out in 2019. It's an artificial intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:31.080
gifted to the supervillain by Lex Luthor that&nbsp;
emulates the thoughts of Black Manta's deceased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:36.233
father. It also doesn't hurt that it's encased in&nbsp;
a giant robot capable of destroying its opponents.

00:06:36.233 --> 00:06:39.120
Granted, it's hard to tell from&nbsp;
the trailer whether it is, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:42.480
an adaptation of the Mecha Manta. It&nbsp;
could just be another random vessel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:45.540
Black Manta uses to wage his war&nbsp;
against sea dwellers. It's worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:49.200
noting that audiences saw Black Manta's&nbsp;
father die in the first "Aquaman" movie.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.117
"Damn you!"

00:06:52.117 --> 00:06:53.793
"Go."

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:59.760
Given how the sequel seems to emphasize the&nbsp;
importance of family, it wouldn't be out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:04.200
the question for dear old dad to come back into&nbsp;
the mix. Then again, there's likely already so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:08.280
much going on in the sequel that adding an AI&nbsp;
layer could make things even more convoluted.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:12.960
The trailer for "Aquaman and the Lost Kingdom"&nbsp;
is filled to the brim with narrative threads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:16.800
that are worth unraveling. While Arthur Curry&nbsp;
is definitely struggling with being a father in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.280
this cinematic outing, he's also dealing&nbsp;
with being a good brother. At the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:23.940
the first "Aquaman," Curry's half-brother&nbsp;
Orm is imprisoned after his gambit to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:27.840
over Atlantis flops. While Curry and&nbsp;
Orm were never on the best of terms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:31.320
the final moments of "Aquaman" cemented&nbsp;
the animosity the two had for one another.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:36.180
In "The Lost Kingdom," Orm is surprisingly back,&nbsp;
and the vibe he shares with Curry is nothing short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:40.560
of hilarious. The trailer shows Curry having&nbsp;
no choice but to break Orm out of prison so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:45.060
they can stop Black Manta together — making&nbsp;
the sequel a true family affair. From there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:48.240
the trailer makes it a point to hammer&nbsp;
home how Orm and Aquaman are on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:51.780
strange journey together, whether&nbsp;
they like it or not. Needless to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:55.620
the bromance vibes are oozing thanks to&nbsp;
the chemistry between Wilson and Momoa.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:59.580
While speaking with Entertainment Weekly,&nbsp;
director James Wan candidly discussed how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:03.540
he always wanted "Aquaman 2" to focus on&nbsp;
the two estranged brothers. He revealed:

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:07.320
"The second movie was always going to&nbsp;
be Arthur and Orm. So, the first was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:11.100
romance action-adventure movie, the second&nbsp;
one is a bromance action-adventure movie."

